# modules.cpp audio interface

# Status and authority

status: official gives the implemented portions of this document the same
authority as the other v1.2.x specifications. This document specifies mm.audio,
the portable audio interface: the In, Out, and Stream interfaces, the Ring
stream, the sample arithmetic, and the selection pair, and mm.audio.es8311,
the portable driver for the ES8311 codec's DAC and ADC. They and their host
tests exist; the ES8311 driver is unqualified on hardware. The board providers
that implement a Microphone and a Speaker -- on the bare Pico boards over paced
ADC capture and a PWM-backed DAC, on the Waveshare RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8 over
I2S, on Linux over ALSA, under SDL over SDL's audio queue -- are specified in
Board providers below as the contract they will be written to, and none exists
yet.

docs/modules-platform-mcu.mdy remains authoritative for the mm.mcu transports
a provider uses, including paced ADC capture, the DAC, and I2S, and their
shared lifecycle. docs/modules-execution.mdy remains authoritative for what a
call may do with the caller's thread. docs/modules-c++20.mdy remains
authoritative for what the code may say. docs/modules-mm.mdy remains
authoritative for the closed v1.2 manifest schema.

# Decision

Audio is a device service with three interfaces and no pins. It is not a
transport, not a group of virtual functions on mm::mcu::Platform, and not a
mixer, a resampler, or an effect engine.

Opening fence
                        application
                         |       ^
                  write  |       |  read
                         v       |
    audio::In  -----> audio::Stream -----> audio::Out        mm.audio
        ^                  ^                    ^
        |                  |                    |
    Microphone         audio::Ring           Speaker         board platform
        |                                       |            provider
        v                                       v
   mm.mcu ADC or I2S                    mm.mcu DAC or I2S     mm.mcu
Closing fence

An application that plays or records sound declares:

Opening fence
use: mm.audio
Closing fence

It names no pin, transport, converter, or provider module. The selected board
supplies those facts.

Sound is continuous and each converter keeps its own clock: a DAC consumes at
its rate whether or not the program is ready, and a microphone produces
whether or not anyone reads. Every other device interface in the tree does one
thing per call, and none of them could say this. The Stream is what absorbs
the difference between the converter's clock and the program's. It has one
producer and one consumer. An Out started on a Stream consumes from it, an In
started on a Stream produces into it, and the application is on the other
end. An In and an Out started on one Stream are connected directly, and the
application only keeps them serviced.

# Portable interface

## Partitions

Opening fence
status.cppm     mm.audio:status     Status
types.cppm      mm.audio:types      Format, Rate, Description, End, Counts
sample.cppm     mm.audio:sample     to_sample, from_sample, and offset forms
stream.cppm     mm.audio:stream     Stream, Ring
platform.cppm   mm.audio:platform   In, Out, set_in, set_out, selected_*
audio.cppm      mm.audio            re-exports the five
Closing fence

mm.audio carries the platform-interface marker and uses nothing but the
standard library. It does not use mm.mcu: an application that only handles
Streams, or a Linux lane whose devices are a sound card, does not reach mm.mcu
through it.

Status has mm.imu's enumerators, unchanged. Every output parameter changes only
on Ok.

## Samples

A sample is a signed sixteen-bit integer, std::int16_t, the only sample type in
this version, and this version is mono: a frame is one sample and every count
is in samples. Floating point is refused for mm.imu's reason: it would be
forced on bare-metal lanes that have no hardware for it, to serve callers that
can convert for themselves. Stereo enters as a channel count in Format,
interleaved frames, and counts in frames; nothing else here depends on there
being one channel.

The sample arithmetic is the only place a bit width appears, and it takes the
width as an argument because which width a transport has is the board's fact.
to_sample shifts a narrower signed value up and keeps a wider one's upper
sixteen bits; to_sample_offset does the same for an offset-binary count, a SAR
converter's, with half of full scale as zero. from_sample and
from_sample_offset are the inverses: widening shifts, narrowing keeps the upper
bits, and silence is zero or half scale. Values outside a width's range
saturate, and a width outside one to thirty-two answers zero. A sample survives
a round trip through any width of sixteen bits or more exactly.

## Rates

Format carries the nominal rate in hertz; Rate carries the exact one as a
numerator and a denominator. A device is configured with the rate a caller asks
for and answers with the Format it runs, the exact rate rounded to the nearest
hertz, and reports the exact rate on request. Starting a device on a Stream
whose Format differs is BadArgument, so an In and an Out at plainly different
rates cannot be joined by accident.

Equal nominal rates are not equal exact rates. On a Pico the ADC is paced from
clk_adc and the DAC from clk_sys, two PLLs locked to one crystal and divided by
dividers that round differently, so a Microphone and a Speaker asked for one
rate generally run at two and the Stream between them slowly fills or empties.
mm.audio reports the rates precisely enough for a caller to compute that slip
and counts what it costs. Correcting it is policy above this module, with
mixing, resampling, volume, and DC removal.

## Stream and Ring

Stream is an interface because a device needs only its contract -- how much
there is, how much room, read, write, claim, and where to count what was lost
-- never its storage. Every method has an honest default. A generator of a tone
or of silence, a file, a test fixture's recorded samples, or a mixer above this
module is a direct subclass, and no device changes for it.

- read and write move up to the span's size and answer the count, which may be
  zero; zero is an answer, not a failure.
- A device claims one end when it starts and releases it when it stops, so two
  devices cannot share an end; a second claim on a held end is Busy. A Stream
  with one meaningful end refuses the other. Claims are made from the thread
  that starts and stops devices.
- underran and overran are written by the device at the end that met the loss,
  and counts reads them.

The execution model gives a program one thread, and no interrupt touches a
Stream. An implementation must still be correct with its two ends in two
contexts, a provider that moves one end inside itself or a hosted test's two
threads, and Ring is.

Ring is the Stream mm.audio ships: a single-producer, single-consumer ring over
storage the caller owns, which must outlive every device started on it.

- configure takes the storage and the Format, refuses empty storage, storage
  too large for the indices, and a zero rate with BadArgument, and is Busy
  while an end is held. The Ring starts empty with zero counts.
- The two indices run from zero to twice the capacity and wrap there, which
  tells full from empty without a wasted slot and without a power-of-two
  capacity. Each is written by one side only, with plain atomic loads and
  stores: no read-modify-write, so nothing needs a lock or runtime support,
  Cortex-M0+ included. The producer publishes with release after copying in,
  the consumer acquires before copying out, and the read index goes back the
  same way.
- write_region and read_region lend the contiguous run available now, up to
  the wrap, for an application that synthesises or consumes in place.
  commit_write and commit_read publish part or all of it; committing more than
  the run now available, including across the wrap, is BadArgument and
  commits nothing. The regions are Ring's rather than Stream's because a
  generator or a socket has no contiguous storage to lend; a device calls
  only read and write.
- reset empties the Ring and zeroes its counts, and is Busy while an end is
  held.
- An unconfigured Ring holds nothing, lends empty regions, and answers
  NotInitialized for every transfer, commit, and claim.
- Ring never allocates, so a bare-metal application gives it a static array.

The application's own read and write are not checked against the claims. An
application that writes into a Ring an In is producing into has broken the
one-producer rule and gets interleaved samples, not a diagnosis.

## In and Out

Opening fence
class Out {
public:
    virtual ~Out() = default;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Description description() const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status initialize();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status configure(const Format&, Format& actual);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status rate(Rate&) const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status start(Stream&);
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status service();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status position(std::uint64_t&) const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status pending(std::size_t&) const;
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status stop();
    [[nodiscard]] virtual Status sleep();
};
Closing fence

In has the same methods without pending. Every one answers Unsupported by
default. They are two classes rather than one with a direction because a
device is one or the other, and a caller holding an Out cannot start it as a
source.

A device's life is initialize, configure, start, service, stop, and start
again:

- configure claims the transport at the nearest rate it holds, writes the
  actual Format on Ok, and is Busy while started. An Out's transport then
  idles outputting silence, and an I2S interface's clocks run from here on.
- Out::start claims the Stream's consumer end and starts playing, with
  position at zero. The first sample plays at the first period after the
  first accepted offer; the silence before it is not an underrun.
- In::start claims the producer end, discards whatever the transport held,
  and starts capture. Nothing converted before start reaches the Stream.
- service moves what can be moved now between the Stream and the transport
  and never waits. A portable application calls it at least once per
  Description::depth_samples; a device that feeds itself answers Ok and does
  nothing.
- position is, for an Out, the samples from the Stream the transport has
  finished outputting since start, excluding silence it inserted; for an In,
  the samples converted since start, including those the transport dropped.
- pending is the samples an Out has taken from the Stream and not yet output.
  Draining is servicing until the Stream is empty and pending is zero.
- stop discards everything below the Stream, returns the transport to idle,
  and releases the end. The Stream keeps what it holds, so start resumes from
  it. stop is immediate and is not a sample-exact pause: a caller that wants
  a gapless pause stops writing, drains, and then stops.

Nothing taken is lost. An Out keeps the run a transport did not accept, offers
it first at the next service, and reads from the Stream again only when
nothing is pending; an error leaves the run for the next call. An In takes
from its transport no more than the Stream has room for, so it never holds a
sample the Stream refused, and when the application falls behind the loss is
the transport's, where it is counted.

Underrun and overrun belong to the transport. A transmitter with nothing
queued outputs silence by itself and counts the period; a receiver with a full
buffer drops and counts. A device reports the growth of those counts to its
Stream, a Speaker's as underrun and a Microphone's as overrun, and that is the
only source of either. A Stream that is merely empty while the transport still
has samples queued is not an underrun, because nothing audible happened.

set_in, selected_in, set_out, and selected_out select the devices. A board's
provider registers its two from a static initialiser. mm.audio's own fallback
In and Out answer Unsupported; they are what a host lane links, and what a
host test replaces with a stand-in.

# Board providers

A board that has audio has one provider module for mm.audio, named
platform.<board>.audio and living under platforms/ with the platform it runs
on, even when the board's manifest lives under boards/: a binding names a
module, not a directory. It exports a Microphone, final, derived from In, and a
Speaker, final, derived from Out, and registers one of each. The pins, the
transport, the slot, the bit width, the rates the hardware holds, and the depth
of its buffering are constants in the provider. A board with a speaker and no
microphone registers a Microphone whose every call answers Unsupported, and
the reverse, so a caller of selected_in or selected_out always gets its answer
from the board.

- Bare Pico boards -- pico, pico-w, pico2-arm, pico2-riscv, pico2-w-arm, and
  pico2-w-riscv -- bind platform.pico.audio in their own manifests, not their
  SDKs', so a board built on another layout does not receive these pins by
  accident. Its Microphone paces ADC channel 0 on GP26 and converts with
  to_sample_offset; its Speaker is the ten-bit PWM-backed DAC output on GP22,
  converted with from_sample_offset, behind two RC stages of 1 kilohm and 10
  nanofarads into a line-level input. A derived board inherits the binding and
  either accepts the pins or rebinds; pad ownership turns a collision into
  Busy at configure.
- The Waveshare RP2350-Touch-LCD-2.8 rebinds mm.audio to
  platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_28.audio, in platforms/pico. Its schematic shows a
  PCM5101A DAC, strapped unmuted and for I2S framing with its system-clock pin
  grounded, feeding an always-on APA2068 amplifier, with BCK on GP2, LRCK on
  GP3, and DIN on GP4. Its Speaker is an Out straight over mm.mcu I2S, since
  the DAC has no control port. It configures sixteen-bit slots at the
  nearest of 32, 44.1, 48, 96, and 192 kHz -- the rates the PCM5101A
  datasheet lists for its PLL from a bit clock of 32 times the rate -- writes
  each sample to both slots, and keeps the clocks running across stop and
  start so the DAC stays locked. Its Microphone answers Unsupported: there is
  none, and GP26 and GP27 are the battery's.
- The Waveshare RP2350 LCD 1.54 family binds
  platform.rp2350_touch_lcd_154.audio, in platforms/pico: rp2350_lcd_154 binds
  it and rp2350_touch_lcd_154 inherits it. Its ES8311 is a full-duplex codec
  on I2S data out GP1, data in GP2, bit clock GP4, and word clock GP5,
  controlled at 0x18 on I2C1, GP6 and GP7. Its
  Speaker is an es8311 Output that also drives the NS4150B amplifier's enable,
  GP0, high only between the codec's unmute and mute; its Microphone is an
  es8311 Input on the analog microphone. Waveshare's firmware makes the codec
  the I2S master from a 6.144 MHz MCLK on GP3; this provider makes it the slave
  the mm.mcu I2S facility drives, with its master clock from the bit clock, and
  holds GP3 low. apps/audio-smoke plays a tone while recording through
  whichever devices a board registers, over the Pico platform's PIO I2S.
- The Linux SDKs bind platform.linux.audio, a Microphone and a Speaker over the
  ALSA kernel interface with no library, their device paths read from the
  device map.
- The SDL boards bind platform.linux.sdl.audio over SDL_QueueAudio and
  SDL_DequeueAudio driven from service.

drafts/plan-audio.mdy records the research, the schematic reading, and the
staged plan these follow.

# Codec drivers

A converter with registers has a portable driver under modules/mm/audio, in
the place mm.imu.qmi8658 holds for the IMU, so every board that carries the
part shares one register sequence. The driver implements In or Out itself,
over mm.mcu, and takes its wiring from the board's provider, which constructs
it and registers it as the board's Microphone or Speaker.

mm.audio.es8311 is the first. It drives the Everest ES8311 as three classes:
Codec, the chip -- identified by its chip registers, 0x83 and 0x11 at 0xfd and
0xfe, controlled at 0x18 or 0x19 over I2C, reset once, and fed over one I2S
link as the platform's slave with its master clock taken from the bit clock
and multiplied to 256 times the rate, so a board needs no MCLK line -- and
Output and Input, its DAC and its ADC, each holding the Codec. They are two
classes because a class cannot be an In and an Out under one level of
dispatch. The link's one word clock is one rate for both: a configure at
another rate moves it only while neither direction is started. Each direction
powers up its own converter muted, unmutes its serial port on start, and mutes
it on stop before discarding the transport's buffer, while the clocks keep
running.

Output keeps the Out contract: the pending run is offered before the Stream is
read, zero and partial acceptance and a transmitter error lose nothing, each
sample goes to both slots, transmitter silence is reported as underrun, and
position and pending come from the transmitter's progress. Input keeps the In
contract: it takes no more than the Stream has room for, reads the analog
microphone's sample from the board's slot at the board's PGA gain, narrows
thirty-two-bit words, and reports receiver drops as overrun. Each Description
depth is the driver's own scratch of sixty-four frames, a lower bound on its
buffering, because mm.mcu does not report the platform's I2S depth.

An earlier version on the audio-work branch addressed the part at 0x06,
programmed the ADC's serial port 0x0a for playback where the DAC's is 0x09,
never enabled the clock manager, the analog section, or the DAC, allocated a
byte vector on every play, sent each sample in one slot of a byte-span link,
and had no input. The driver here corrects each of these.

# Conformance

- The sample arithmetic answers zero for widths outside one to thirty-two; maps
  every width's most negative value to the most negative sample and zero to
  silence; centres an offset count on half scale; shifts narrow values up and
  keeps wide values' upper sixteen bits; saturates out-of-range values; and
  round-trips a sample exactly through every width of sixteen bits or more.
- An unconfigured Ring holds nothing and moves, lends, commits, and claims
  nothing. configure refuses empty storage and a zero rate, and a held end
  makes configure and reset Busy.
- One device claims each end; a second claim is Busy; a released end can be
  claimed again.
- For every capacity from one to nine and every starting offset, every write
  length that fits is accepted whole and read back in order.
- A full Ring accepts nothing and an empty one gives nothing, each answering
  Ok with a zero count.
- The regions lend the run up to the wrap, and a commit past the run,
  including one across the wrap with the whole Ring free, commits nothing.
- The two loss counts accumulate separately and reset zeroes them.
- A producer thread and a consumer thread pass three million samples through
  a ring of sixty-one, the producer by copy and the consumer in place, and
  every sample arrives once, in order, with nothing left over; a side that
  stops making progress fails the case rather than hanging the run.
- A bare Stream, In, and Out answer Unsupported and leave every output
  parameter alone; a Stream that is not a Ring stands in for one through the
  interface alone.
- With no provider, the selection is the fallback; a registered device is the
  one selected, and calls reach it.

- The ES8311 driver identifies the part and refuses another; resets it once
  however many directions initialise; writes the shared, DAC, and ADC
  transcripts in order for both slot widths; refuses a rate outside eight to
  ninety-six kilohertz, a direction with no data line for it, a gain past ten
  steps, and a twenty-four-bit slot; runs both directions at one rate and
  moves the link only while neither is started.
- Its Output sends each sample in both slots, widened for thirty-two-bit
  slots; keeps its pending run through zero acceptance, partial acceptance,
  and a transmitter error, reading the Stream only when nothing is pending;
  reports transmitter silence as underrun growth; and on stop mutes, discards
  the queue, keeps the clocks, releases the end, and resumes on restart.
- Its Input captures only after start, from the wired slot, narrowing
  thirty-two-bit words; takes no more than the Stream holds, so every sample
  arrives once and in order; reports receiver drops as overrun growth, with
  position counting them; leaves the Stream alone on a receiver error; and on
  stop mutes and keeps what the Stream holds, a restart discarding what the
  receiver held.

tests/mm/audio and tests/mm/audio/es8311 pin these. No hardware is involved.

# What this does not settle

Whether Ring's application-side read and write should refuse an end a device
holds, which would turn silent corruption into a diagnosis for one atomic load
per call. Whether a pause that keeps what is below the Stream is worth adding
to the transports. Whether a paced ADC should permit interleaved single reads,
which would let a board read its battery while capturing at the cost of a gap
in the capture. The name Stream sits beside mm::sketch::Stream, which means
something else; a sketch that imports both modules qualifies one.
